*The results on this page were computed when the vignette was written
(see `vignettes/precompile.R`); the code is what produced them.*

```{r setup}
library(spice)
```

One deck, four analyses: a common-emitter stage with an emitter bypass,
looked at the way a designer would — where it sits, how much it
amplifies and up to where, how much noise it adds, and what it does to
a signal that is too large.

## The circuit

<img src="figures/circuit-amplifier.svg" alt="Schematic of the common-emitter amplifier: divider bias R1 and R2, collector resistor Rc, emitter resistor Re bypassed by Ce, input through Rs and Cin, output through Cout into Rl." />

```{r deck}
amp <- "common-emitter amplifier
vcc vcc 0 12
vin in 0 dc 0 ac 1 sin(0 10m 1k)
rs in b1 1k
cin b1 b 10u
r1 vcc b 47k
r2 b 0 10k
q1 c b e qn
rc vcc c 4.7k
re e 0 1k
ce e 0 100u
cout c out 10u
rl out 0 10k
.model qn npn(is=1e-14 bf=200 vaf=100 cje=4p cjc=2p tf=0.3n rb=50)
.op
.ac dec 20 1 100meg
.noise v(out) vin dec 10 10 1meg
.tran 10u 4m
.end"
res <- spice_run(amp)
res
```

## Bias

`spice_op()` gives the operating point as a named vector.

```{r op}
res |> spice_op(c("b", "e", "c"))
```

The collector sits near mid-supply, with about 1.4 mA through the
transistor (`v(e)` across the 1 kOhm emitter resistor).

## Gain and bandwidth

The AC plot is complex; its tidy table carries the magnitude, the gain
in dB and the phase in degrees. `.measure`
expressions work on a finished plot too, through `spice_measure()`.

```{r ac}
out <- res |> spice_tidy("ac", "out")

ggplot(out, aes(scale, db)) +
  geom_line(colour = "#b8741a", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  labs(x = "frequency (Hz)", y = "gain (dB)", title = "Voltage gain, v(out)/vin")
```

```{r band}
acp <- spice_plots(res)[["AC Analysis"]]
m <- spice_measure(acp, c(
  peak = "max vdb(out)",
  f_lo = "when vdb(out)=38.4 rise=1",
  f_hi = "when vdb(out)=38.4 fall=1"
))
m[, c("name", "value")]
```

About 41 dB in the passband; the lower corner is set by the emitter
bypass capacitor, the upper one by the transistor's capacitances
against the source resistance.

## Noise

`.noise` gives the output noise density and the same referred to the
input, and their integrals over the sweep.

```{r noise}
nz <- spice_tidy(res, "noise")
ggplot(nz, aes(scale, value * 1e9, colour = variable)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_x_log10() + scale_y_log10() +
  labs(x = "frequency (Hz)", y = "noise density (nV/sqrt(Hz))", colour = NULL)

spice_tidy(res, "inoise")[, c("variable", "value")]
```

## A signal that is too large

The deck's transient drives 10 mV; the stage is linear there. Sixty
millivolts is another matter. A session keeps the circuit built and
lets one alter a source and run again.

```{r clip}
s <- spice_session(amp)
run_at <- function(amplitude) {
  spice_alter(s, "vin", sin = c(0, amplitude, 1e3))
  tr <- spice_rerun(s) |> spice_tidy("tran", "out")
  tr <- tr[, c("scale", "value")]
  tr$drive <- sprintf("%g mV", amplitude * 1e3)
  tr
}
both <- rbind(run_at(10e-3), run_at(60e-3))

ggplot(both, aes(scale * 1e3, value, colour = drive)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.7) +
  labs(x = "time (ms)", y = "v(out) (V)", colour = "input",
       title = "The stage clips on the way down")
```
